🔥 1. Parallel Streams — WHY THEY ARE DANGEROUS
🔹 What is a Parallel Stream?
list.parallelStream()

Uses ForkJoinPool.commonPool
Splits data across multiple threads
Works best for CPU-bound, stateless operations

⚠️ DANGER #1: Shared Mutable State (MOST COMMON BUG)
❌ WRONG
List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
list.parallelStream().forEach(result::add); // ❌
Why?
ArrayList is NOT thread-safe
Data corruption / missing elements
✅ FIX
List<Integer> result =
    list.parallelStream().collect(Collectors.toList());

📌 Interview line
“Parallel streams must not mutate shared state.”

⚠️ DANGER #2: Order Is NOT Guaranteed
list.parallelStream().forEach(System.out::print);

Output order = unpredictable

If order matters:
list.parallelStream().forEachOrdered(System.out::print);

⚠️ But forEachOrdered kills performance.

⚠️ DANGER #3: Blocking Calls in Parallel Streams

❌ BAD

list.parallelStream().forEach(this::callDB);


Why?

ForkJoinPool has limited threads

Blocking = thread starvation

Can hang entire JVM

📌 Golden rule

Never use parallel streams for I/O, DB, REST calls.

⚠️ DANGER #4: findFirst() vs findAny()
parallelStream().findFirst(); // slow
parallelStream().findAny();   // fast


Why?

findFirst() preserves order → synchronization

findAny() returns first available → no coordination

🎯 Senior Interview Answer

“Parallel streams are powerful but risky. I only use them for stateless, CPU-heavy operations and never for I/O or shared mutable state.”
